4 0 obj
<< /Length 1116 >>
stream
BT /F2 10 Tf 50 750 Td (Syllora Academic Repository | Course: CS301 Operating Systems) Tj 400 0 Td (Page 2 of 4) Tj -400 -25 Td /F2 16 Tf (CS301: Silberschatz - Operating System Concepts \(Ch. 8\)) Tj 0 -30 Td /F1 11 Tf 16 TL T* /F2 13 Tf (8.2 Deadlock Characterization: The Four Coffman Conditions) Tj /F1 11 Tf T* (A deadlock situation can arise if and only if the following four conditions hold) Tj T* (simultaneously in a concurrent computing system:) Tj T* (1. Mutual Exclusion: At least one resource must be held in a nonsharable mode.) Tj T* (2. Hold and Wait: A process must be simultaneously holding at least one resource) Tj T* (   and actively requesting additional resources held by other executing processes.) Tj T* (3. No Preemption: Resources cannot be forcibly preempted; a resource can be released) Tj T* (   only voluntarily by the process holding it after that process completes its task.) Tj T* (4. Circular Wait: A closed set {P0, P1, ..., Pn} of waiting processes must exist such that) Tj T* (   P0 is waiting for a resource held by P1, P1 is waiting for P2, and Pn is waiting for P0.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
5 0 obj
<< /Length 1347 >>
stream
BT /F2 10 Tf 50 750 Td (Syllora Academic Repository | Course: CS301 Operating Systems) Tj 400 0 Td (Page 3 of 4) Tj -400 -25 Td /F2 16 Tf (CS301: Silberschatz - Operating System Concepts \(Ch. 8\)) Tj 0 -30 Td /F1 11 Tf 16 TL T* /F2 13 Tf (8.3 Resource-Allocation Graphs & Deadlock vs Starvation) Tj /F1 11 Tf T* /F2 10 Tf (    "A deadlock occurs when every process in a set is waiting for an event that only another process in the set can cause.") Tj /F1 11 Tf T* /F2 10 Tf (    "In contrast, starvation \(indefinite postponement\) occurs when a runnable process is perpetually denied access because resources are continuously allocated to higher-priority processes.") Tj /F1 11 Tf T* (It is vital to distinguish deadlock from starvation:) Tj T* (- Deadlock represents a cyclic dependency where progress is mathematically impossible without intervention.) Tj T* (- Starvation represents an unfair scheduling outcome where progress is delayed indefinitely,) Tj T* (  even though no circular wait exists and the system as a whole continues executing tasks.) Tj T* (If a resource allocation graph contains cycles and each resource type has only one instance,) Tj T* (then a deadlock has definitely occurred. With multiple instances per resource type, a cycle) Tj T* (indicates a potential deadlock, but not a guaranteed deadlock condition.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
6 0 obj
